  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 111 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows East Idaho within northwest flow regime\nbehind departing shortwave. Orographic flow under weak cyclonic\nlift in the base of the associated closed low over southern \nSaskatchewan is continuing to produce light snows over the \neastern highlands. The low continues to shift east through the \nday, gradually shifting light QPF focus northeast to the Island \nPark region before tapering it off entirely. Upper flow shifts \nto a weak transitory ridge for tonight, then the next shortwave \nfeature lifts northeast across the PacNW Sunday into Monday. \nLight snow showers start in the central mountains during the day\nSunday, but spread eastward into the rest of the forecast area \nlate Sunday night into Monday. Forecast accumulations even at \nhigh elevations with this event are only 1-3\". Model spreads via\nboth NBM and REFS are tight, leading to higher confidence in \nexpectations for minimal impacts with this system. \n\nMoving toward midweek and with an eye toward Thanksgiving\nholiday travel concerns, GFS rebuilds a ridge for Tuesday, so\ndry and continued cool conditions are forecast for all of East\nIdaho. Another system digs into the PacNW for Wednesday, pushing\nprecipitation into western portions of the state. It is\nuncertain how far east the precipitation will progress, as the\ndeterministic runs differ on how the next shortwave ejects out\nof the offshore PacNW low. The GFS has trended toward a split\nflow with the southern shortwave digging well south and taking\nthe moisture with it through Wednesday night, whereas the ECMWF\nholds on to a more consolidated trough moving through Idaho,\nspreading light precipitation across East Idaho Wednesday night\nand Thanksgiving day. Looking at the ensembles, GEFS means\nappear to support the ECMWF solution, as does the NBM. Mean\n24-hr snowfall amounts through Thanksgiving day are light, only\na few inches at higher elevations, and 50th percentile even\nless. Daytime highs in lower elevations are forecast to stay\nabove freezing, so precipitation is expected to be a rain/snow\nmix with minimal accumulation for most. The high end amounts per\nNBM could be impactful Thanksgiving Day in the mountains,\nespecially for passes. For the post-holiday weekend return\ntravel, there are some significant differences in the ensemble\nclusters with respect to building, then keeping, a ridge in\nplace. We will be keeping a close eye on potential holiday\nimpacts as they approach.",
